~Ninaro User Manual Ninaro User Manual Ninaro User Manual 

~Ninaro

Ninaro stands for N-in-a-row, which means that the connect
four game has been extended to connect any number on any
sized grid. Logically however a minimum of 3 rows, 3cols,
3 in a row (3x3x3) applies. All the rules about the game
connect-4 still apply. It is still a two player game, one
feature of Ninaro being that you can play computer
opponents, known as AI's (Artificial Intelligences).

~Table of Help Keys
Key         Function
Left        Scrolls UP a page
Right       Scrolls DOWN a page
Up          Scrolls UP a line
Down        Scrolls DOWN a line
PageUp      Scrolls UP a page
PageDown    Scrolls DOWN a page
Home        Scrolls to the top of help
End         Scrolls to the end of help

~How To Play

The aim of the game is to connect a number of tokens in the
grid while stopping your opponent from doing so. The first
player to make the connection wins. The number of tokens
you must connect to win is shown at the top right of the
screen, under the Connect heading. Connections can take
place in four different ways, being vertical, horizontal,
and the two diagonals. A connection occurs when a token is
next to a token of the same type. A win occurs when a
number of tokens are connected in this way. When a win
occurs, the game prompts you to press Cancel to continue.

~Controlling Ninaro

Ninaro is controlled with either the keyboard or the mouse.
Only player one can use the mouse while in the game, any
player can use the mouse in the menus or dialogs.

~Player's Keys

Each player has a set of keys, consisting of Left, Right,
Up, Down, Select and Cancel. The table below shows these
keys.

~Table of Player Keys
Key/Player      Player One      Player Two
Left            A               Keypad 4
Right           D               Keypad 6
Up              W               Keypad 8
Down            S               Keypad 2
Select          E               ENTER
Cancel          Q               BackSpace

The keypad keys can be used as either the grey arrow keys
or the numbered keys on the numerical keypad for which
numlock must be off.

~What The Keys Do.

Depending on where you are in the program, the keys do
different things, though they are quite similar. The table
below shows the functionality of the keys at each location
on the program.

~Table of Key Functionality
Key/Location    Playing A Game  Using Menu    Using Dialogs
Left            Token Left      Left a Menu   Left an item
Right           Token Right     Right a Menu  Right an item
Up              Calls the menu  Up an item    Up a group
Down            Drops Token     Down an item  Down a group
Select          Drops Token     Select item   Select Button-
                                             OK,Reset,Cancel
Cancel          Calls the menu  Cancel Menu   Cancels Dialog

~The Mouse

Only Player One has access to the mouse while playing a
game. The following table shows mouse functionality.

~Table of Mouse Functionality
Key     Mouse Action
Left    Move mouse left
Right   Move mouse right
Up      Move mouse up
Down    Move mouse down
Select  Press Left Mouse Button
Cancel  Press Right Mouse Button

To enter a token move the mouse to the appropriate column
in the grid and press Select (left-click). Obviously using
the right mouse button as Cancel calls the menu.

~The Menus

The menus are the key way in Ninaro of changing things. The
menu is accessible at almost anytime by pressing ESC, M for
menu, or your appropriate player key.

~The Game Menu

The game menu lets you control the game.

New     starts a new game
Load    loads the saved game, Load Error appears if no save
        file found
Save    saves the game, there is only one saved game
End     ends the current game, when no game is on all menu
        options are accessible (e.g. player one and two)
        dialogs.
Suggest suggests a move to make. The move is suggested by
Move    AI Genius.
Toggle  toggles the sound On and Off
Sound
Options Allows you to change the background and hilite
        colours
Exit    exits the game. 


~The Board Menu

The board menu lets you change the board attributes

7x8x4   Changes the board to 7 Columns, 8 Rows,Connect 4
12x13x6 Changes the board to 12 Columns,13 Rows,Connect 6
Custom  Changes the board via The Custom Board Dialog

~The Player Menu

The player menu lets you change the player attributes

One...  Changes the attributes of player one
        via The Player Dialog
Two...  Changes the attributes of player two
        via The Player Dialog
Options Changes the player options
        via The Player Options Dialog
Reset   resets both player's scores to zero.
Scores

~The Help Menu

The help menu provides all your help needs, via this screen.
Help is available at anytime by pressing either F1 or H.

Contents    Brings up the help screen, scrolled to the top
About       Brings up the about window


~The Dialogs

Dialogs help you choose from a group of options. In the
dialogs C cancels, R resets and O ok's. Pressing Select
at any time while in the dialog results on the hilited
button (OK,Reset,Cancel) being used, while pressing cancel
cancels the dialog. The options chosen in a dialog only
take effect if the OK button is pressed. This means that
any number of changes can be made while inside a dialog,
but none of these changes will take effect if the Cancel
button is pressed.  Reset resets the dialog to have the
values it had when the dialog was first opened.

~The Custom Board Dialog

The custom board dialog allows you to change the size of
the board and/or the number of tokens which must be
connected to win a game. You will notice that an incorrect
value can never be selected, as the game size automatically
resizes when the number of columns or rows is changed.

~The Player Dialog

The player dialog allows you to change a player's colour
and/or name. If a player's name is changed, a new game is
started (as a new player has entered the game). If however
only a player's colour is changed, the tokens in the game
change colour and the game continues. You will notice that
you cannot select the colour that your opponent has. Should
you wish to have their colour, they must first change their
colour to something else, then you may change to be the
colour which is now free.

~The Player Options Dialog

The player options dialog allows the game to be customized
to suit your needs. There are six ways that a player may be
chosen by to start the game, and the AI speed may also be
adjusted to suit your needs. If both players are AI's and
the AI speed is set to fastest, the games will proceed
without prompting the user to hit escape to continue when a
game is run. In this way the game can be set on demo mode.

~The Game Options Dialog

The game options dialog allows you to customize some of the
colours used in the game. The background colour can be set
as either black or white, and the hilite colour can be
selected from a list of colour alternatives.

~Troubleshooting

~I don't want to be YELLOW anymore.
You can change your colour via the Player Options Dialog,
which is accessed through the menu from the Player Menu.
Should you wish to be the colour that you opponent has,
see the next heading.

~My opponent is RED and I want to be RED.
You have two options, convince the other player to be
another colour and then choose RED, or choose another
colour. AI opponents can be very easily convinced, just
change their colour.

~I want to play someone else.
If you are currently playing a game, Call the menu and use
Game->End. Change the other player by using the Player Menu,
selecting the opponent (If you are player one, select player
two and visa versa). Change the name to someone else, and
press OK. Call the menu again and use Game->New.

~I want the loser to go first always.
Call the menu, use Player->Options, and change the Goes
First to loser.

~I don't know which move to make, help me decide!
If you are unsure about which move to make, call the menu
and use Game->Suggest Move. AI Genius will then move the
token to the column in which it would make a move.

~I am too good, who else can I play?
You can either play a harder AI, play a human, or wait for
the next version which will have better AI's.

~The sound is really bugging me, can I turn it off?
The sound can be toggled by Game->Toggle Sound. The current
status of the sound can be seen at the top right of the
screen under the heading 'Sound'. If it is On, toggle the
sound to off.

~Someone made the hilite RED, I prefer GREEN!
The hilite colour can be changed via Game->Options. Change
the hilite from RED to GREEN via the dialog which is
presented.

~The configuration is bad, I prefered the default one.
Simply delete the NINARO.CFG file which is in the game
directory. When ninaro finds no configuration file,
it creates a default one.

~The computer is too slow, can I speed it up?
If the computer is too slow, call the menu and use
Player->Options. Change the AI speed to the desired setting.

~The computer is too fast, can I slow it down?
If the computer is too fast, call the menu and use
Player->Options. Change the AI speed to the desired setting.

~I am addicted to the game.
This is probably because you win enough games to make it
worthwhile. Make the game harder, refer to the paragraph
below for more information. If this is still not a solution,
then as the creator of this game I have won!!! Wait for the
next version which will have harder opponents.

~I am totally undefeated, the game is no challenge.
Try changing some of the following :-
Player->Options->Goes First as Loser
Board->Custom->(Change the connect size). Higher levels of
connection are harder to obtain.
Board->Custom->(Change the row or column size). Different
boards can have different strategies.
Play a human. Humans are better in some areas of strategy
than AI's. Humans learn from their mistakes.

~Tutorial

An important concept about the game is that you need to be
in a game to play. If you are in a game you can end it by
using Game->End. This then gives you full access to all
menu items(except End), meaning you can change both players.
Once you are ready to start a game with the options you have
selected, use Game->New.

~Typical Game.

Gavin and Ronda would like to play a game.  The game is
currently configured as Player One vs AI Genius.  They are
playing a game. Ronda calls up the menu by pressing ESC,
uses Game->End. She then calls up the menu and uses
Player->Two. She changes the player to Human and presses OK.
She then calls up the menu and uses Game->New. The game is
on.
Orville walks into the room, and Ronda asks him to play
for her. Gavin agrees. Orville looks at the screen, Gavin
is GREEN and Ronda is Red. He says -'all the tokens are the
same colour!!'. Gavin, realising that Orville is RED-GREEN
colour blind, calls up the menu and uses Player->Two. He
changes Orville's colour to ORANGE. The then proceeds as
though nothing had happened. All the RED tokens are now
ORANGE.
Later on, Orville realises the reason he is losing is
because Ronda was losing when he took over. He calls Gavin's
attention to this, and the call up the menu and use
Player->Reset Scores. The now have zero games each won, and
can fairly play again. Gavin Wins a game. Orville, seeing
where the connection had occurred, presses Cancel and they
continue.

~About Ninaro

Ninaro was written by the author in 1998.
The project was supervised by the coordinator. 

